  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nHigh pressure centered across the Ohio Valley and Mid-Atlantic \ncontinues dry, calm and quiet weather this afternoon and \nthrough Wednesday. Mostly sunny skies broken by fair weather CU \nwill continue this afternoon, with the diurnal CU fading with \nthe loss of heating after sunset. Another night of clear skies,\nlight winds and small dewpoint depressions yields another \nmorning of expected river valley fog early Wednesday. \n\nSubtly rising heights allow for a slight bump up in daytime \nhighs on Wednesday with mid 80s expected across the lowlands. \nLower dewpoints keep humidity levels in the comfortable range.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nOur next active weather maker is an upper trough that swings \ninto the Ohio Valley from the northwest late Wednesday night\ninto Thursday. Showers and storms are expected to be ongoing \nacross the western Ohio Valley Wednesday afternoon but \nconvection is expected to be fizzling as it approaches our Ohio \ncounties given a loss of daytime heating with an expected \nevening arrival time. \n\nA moist southerly flow and moisture pooling ahead of the cold \nfront helps dewpoints surge into the upper 60s to low 70s \nThursday. Some uncertainty comes with how much heating we can \nattain with expected rather stout cloud cover. Despite this \nuncertainty, models prog between 1500-2500 J/kg of mixed layer \nCAPE in the area. Bulk shear estimates range from 20-30 kts but\nincrease east of the spine of the Appalachians, where the days \ngreatest severe threat may be seen. DCAPE values up to 1000 J/kg\nand steep low level lapse rates support a damaging wind threat.\nSome dry air exists in the mid- levels but lower lapse rates \nand rather high freezing levels may cap our hail risk some. All \nin all, our convective environment supports multicell clusters \nwith a possible transient supercell structure or two and points \nlargely to a damaging wind threat, although some marginally \nsevere hail can't be ruled out, especially in any supercell\nstructures. At this time eastern portions of the state are in \nthe D3 Marginal Risk of severe weather from SPC.\n\nGreater moisture flux up the I-95 corridor significantly raises\nPWATS east of us and as such keeps most hydro concerns out of \nour area. 90th percentile PWATs put us in the 1.60-1.80 inch \nrange, a far cry from some of our recent rainfall events. This \nalong with a decent expected forward speed keeps our\nprobabilities of greater than an inch of rainfall on the lower \nend. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\n\nLobes of vorticity rounding the base of the lingering upper \nlevel trough likely push a secondary moisture starved cold \nfront through the region on Friday. A lingering shower or two \nacross the lowlands looks possible but the eastern mountains \nhave a shot at another round of showers and storms with uplift \nand convergence maximized along the terrain. \n\nSurface high pressure settles across the region by Friday night\nas building upper-level ridging amplifies across the central \nCONUS. This places us in a largely dry and calm northwest flow \nregime. High temperatures moderate some Friday behind the first \ncold front but then steadily rising heights raise temperatures \neach day through the weekend. By Sunday, low 90s are sneaking \nback into the lowlands.",
